        "excerpt": "mean, you you also hinted at the idea that we kind of anthropomorphize, that we project human like experiences onto animals. We don't know what it's like for them to suffer. I think that cuts both ways though. I mean, it could be the case that animals just kind of don't really feel pain in the way that we do. That's what C.S. Lewis suggested. That's what William Lane Craig has suggested in the past. They seem to have backtracked recently on that. Um but it goes the other way too. I think that dogs experience smell far more acutely than we could possibly imagine. I think that hawks have much better eyesight than we do. Like they experience eyesight more precisely and more acutely than we could imagine. And why is that? Well, because they rely on those senses more than we do. And I feel like wild animals rely on their crude sensory pain receptors much more than we do too with our reason. We don't need them as much. I think there's a good evolutionary reason. If we're going to accept that we should take seriously this this possible experiential gap between us and animals, there's a better argument to make that those animals experience more pain, more acutely, more intensely than we do. And then well, you have have even more of a problem with that."
